= Public good =

== Overview ==
'''Public good''' is a good whose use is substantially non-rival and from which exclusion is difficult or costly under the relevant technological and institutional conditions.

Non-rivalry means one person's use does not substantially reduce the amount available to others. Non-excludability means it is difficult or costly to prevent non-payers or non-members from benefiting.

Public goods need not be produced by governments, and government-produced goods need not be public goods in the economic sense. The classification concerns properties of consumption and exclusion, not ownership alone.

Non-excludability can create free-rider incentives because a person may benefit without contributing. Actual provision can come from governments, communities, firms, philanthropy, norms, bundled services, or other institutions.

== Boundaries ==
* public good ≠ government-provided good
* public good ≠ commons/common-pool resource
* non-rivalry ≠ unlimited capacity under every condition

== Related concepts ==
[[Collective action]], [[Commons]], [[Incentive]], [[Governance]], [[Game theory]], and [[Mechanism design]] clarify provision problems.

== Digital information goods and computational systems ==
In an information service, the ability to copy a digital file without depriving another reader of the original exemplifies potential '''non-rival consumption of the informational content'''. It does not imply that serving copies is costless: storage, bandwidth, curation, security, software maintenance and attention are limited resources. Access can also be restricted through authentication, licensing or technical controls. Public-good status must therefore be evaluated for a specified good and access arrangement, not inferred from the word “digital”.

For [[Related to::Mechanism design|mechanism design]] and multi-agent coordination, the technical question is how individual contributions, exclusion rules, monitoring and incentives affect the provision of a shared informational resource. A software repository, an open dataset and a privately hosted API may have very different institutional and economic characteristics.

BITwiki studies this concept where it explains concrete '''information infrastructure, provision mechanisms or collective contributions'''. It does not prescribe a particular political system or automatically equate every commons with a pure public good. See [[Collective action]] and [[Commons]].

